    "content": "Artemis II is a 10-day crewed mission scheduled to launch no earlier than April 2026 (per recent April 1, 2026 updates, it launched on that date) on the Space Launch System (SLS) rocket from Kennedy Space Center. It will test the Orion spacecraft with four astronauts, marking the first human return to the moon's vicinity since 1972. Key Mission Timeline (10-Day Mission) Launch: SLS lifts off from Kennedy Space Center, placing the crew in an initial high-Earth orbit for testing. Day 1: Orion performs a manual, human-piloted proximity operations test (Proximity Operations Demonstration) to ensure the spacecraft handles well in space. Days 2–4: The service module fires for a translunar injection (TLI), placing the craft on a figure-eight trajectory toward the Moon. Days 5–6 (Lunar Flyby): The crew will travel roughly 4,600 miles beyond the lunar far side, using it for a gravity-assisted return. They will take photos of the far side and test systems. Days 7–9: Transit back to Earth. Day 10 (Reentry & Splashdown): The crew module separates from the service module and enters the atmosphere at 3,000∘F, landing in the Pacific Ocean with a parachute-assisted splashdown. Astronaut Crew Reid Wiseman (NASA): Commander Victor Glover (NASA): Pilot Christina Koch (NASA): Mission Specialist Jeremy Hansen (CSA): Mission Specialist",
